  "cards": [
    {
      "dot": "cyan",
      "title": "Edge and routing",
      "items": [
        "Clients reach one entry point: the Spring Cloud Gateway on :8080. It verifies the JWT, checks the Redis token blacklist and applies per-user Redis rate limits.",
        "Routes: account (auth, kyc, accounts, users), orchestrator (transfers, reversals, interbank), compliance, notification (REST + /ws), plus pass-through routes to CBS /api/v1/cbs, risk /api/v1/risk and the MailHog API."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "emerald",
      "title": "Dependencies not drawn as arrows",
      "items": [
        "Redis is also used by account (sessions, OTP), orchestrator (idempotency lock, biometric challenge, cool-off), CBS, notification and compliance (ShedLock).",
        "Oracle XE is shared by account, notification and CBS in dev; on AKS the same schemas live in Azure SQL. Notification also persists to it.",
        "Compliance writes generated reports to Azurite. Orchestrator calls account for limits and publishes failed transfers to banking.transfers.dlq. Every JVM and Python service sends traces to the Datadog agent."
      ]
    },
    {
      "dot": "amber",
      "title": "Ledger and audit path",
      "items": [
        "CBS posts OFS transfers and reversals on the master DB with pessimistic row locks, then writes an outbox row and publishes to banking.transfers.events.",
        "CBS also consumes that topic (group cbs-audit-workers) and appends Merkle-hashed rows to the PostgreSQL audit vault. Notification and compliance consume the same topic.",
        "Dev tools (Redis Insight, Kafka UI, Adminer, Azurite Drive) exist only in the local compose stack."
      ]
    }
  ]
}
